WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.665
Two dashboards show live FX rates from one provider.

2
00:00:06.665 --> 00:00:13.145
A subscribes at once, B joins 250 milliseconds later, and a new rate comes every 100.

3
00:00:13.267 --> 00:00:16.146
First the feed is cold, like an HTTP call.

4
00:00:16.146 --> 00:00:20.533
What does B get, and how many connections does the provider see?

5
00:00:22.533 --> 00:00:25.817
A cold publisher runs again for every subscriber.

6
00:00:25.817 --> 00:00:30.039
B gets its own connection, and its own run from the first rate.

7
00:00:30.039 --> 00:00:32.452
The provider sends every tick twice.

8
00:00:33.167 --> 00:00:36.781
share() makes it hot: one run, shared by everyone.

9
00:00:36.781 --> 00:00:37.937
What does B get?

10
00:00:39.933 --> 00:00:43.918
B joins late, so it gets only what comes after: 3 and 4.

11
00:00:43.918 --> 00:00:44.844
It's live TV.

12
00:00:44.844 --> 00:00:47.761
Switch on late, and you missed the start.

13
00:00:48.367 --> 00:00:52.891
replay(1).refCount() also shares one run, but keeps the last item.

14
00:00:52.891 --> 00:00:54.604
cache() keeps every item.

15
00:00:54.604 --> 00:00:56.387
What does B get from each?

16
00:00:58.367 --> 00:01:02.768
With replay(1), B gets 2 the moment it joins, then the live ones.

17
00:01:04.933 --> 00:01:07.161
With cache, B gets 1 and 2 at once.

18
00:01:07.161 --> 00:01:10.788
But cache keeps every item for as long as the Flux lives.

19
00:01:10.788 --> 00:01:12.697
On a long feed, that's a leak.

20
00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:17.986
Sometimes the values come from ordinary code, like a Kafka listener.

21
00:01:17.986 --> 00:01:25.153
A sink lets it push them into a Flux: the listener calls tryEmitNext, and subscribers see sink.asFlux().

22
00:01:25.267 --> 00:01:30.012
A multicast sink sends each item to the subscribers present at that moment.

23
00:01:30.012 --> 00:01:33.365
So it's hot, and B misses what came before it joined.

24
00:01:34.133 --> 00:01:38.074
Now two listener threads call tryEmitNext at the same moment.

25
00:01:38.074 --> 00:01:40.077
What happens to the two events?

26
00:01:42.067 --> 00:01:42.927
One goes in.

27
00:01:42.927 --> 00:01:47.943
The other call gets FAIL_NON_SERIALIZED, and its event is not emitted.

28
00:01:47.943 --> 00:01:52.028
If you ignore the result, events vanish with no log line.

29
00:01:52.133 --> 00:01:55.889
So check every result, or call emitNext with busyLooping.

30
00:01:55.889 --> 00:01:59.315
It spins until the sink is free, for up to a second.

31
00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:04.117
Here are the shared feed and the sink, in Java and in Kotlin.

32
00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:08.458
Cold runs the work again for each subscriber.

33
00:02:08.458 --> 00:02:14.045
Hot runs it once and shares it, and the operator decides what a late subscriber gets.

34
00:02:14.045 --> 00:02:17.595
A sink is a hot source that ordinary code pushes into.

35
00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:18.645
Quick check.

36
00:02:18.645 --> 00:02:24.711
With replay(2).refCount(), what does B get when it joins at 250 milliseconds?
